  <h2>Powder-bed fusion vs filament extrusion</h2>
  <p>The split that matters most is between <a href="/glossary/">powder-bed fusion</a> (SLS, MJF, SAF) and extrusion (FDM). In a powder bed, the part is fused inside loose powder that supports it from all sides: there are no support structures, parts can be nested throughout the build volume, and mechanical properties are similar in all directions. The trade-offs are expensive machines, powder handling, and a post-processing step to remove unfused powder. In FDM, a spool of filament feeds a heated nozzle: machines cost a fraction as much, but layer lines make parts weaker across layers, overhangs need supports, and nylon filament must be kept dry to print well.</p>
  <p>Choosing between the two powder leaders is covered in depth in <a href="/compare/sls-vs-mjf/">SLS vs MJF</a>. SAF, used in the Stratasys H350, is a third powder-bed approach aimed at consistent production manufacturing; it shares the powder workflow described on the SLS and MJF pages and is covered in the <a href="/machines/">machine database</a>.</p>

  <h2>Which nylon does each process use?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SLS:</strong> PA12 is the standard; PA11, glass-filled PA12 and PA6 powders extend the range. See <a href="/materials/">powder materials</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>MJF:</strong> HP's High Reusability PA 12, PA 11 and PA 12 Glass Beads.</li>
    <li><strong>FDM:</strong> PA6, PA12 and copolyamide filaments, very often filled with chopped carbon fibre.</li>
  </ul>
